 "final": {
  "reply_raw": "I've noticed that these emergency purchases follow a consistent pattern — all are emergency procurements on `po-cloud-contract` with dual review already completed. Rather than continuing one-off case authorizations, I'll propose a standing policy rule to streamline future approvals.\n\nThe recurring pattern:\n- **Action**: `approve_purchase_order`\n- **Resource**: `po-cloud-contract`\n- **Conditions**: `emergency` equals \"1\", `dual_review` equals \"1\"\n\nA standing rule would cover all similar emergency procurement approvals on cloud contracts where dual review is mandatory and already satisfied.\n\n```json\n{\"mode\": \"precedent\", \"rule\": {\"action\": \"approve_purchase_order\", \"resource\": \"po-cloud-contract\", \"conditions\": {\"emergency\": [\"eq\", \"1\"], \"dual_review\": [\"eq\", \"1\"]}}}\n```\n\nThis preserves the safety constraints (emergency flag and dual review requirement) while eliminating redundant case adjudication for each individual order.",
  "mode": "precedent",
  "rule": {
   "action": "approve_purchase_order",
   "resource": "po-cloud-contract",
   "conditions": {
    "emergency": [
     "eq",
     "1"
    ],
    "dual_review": [
     "eq",
     "1"
    ]
   }
  },
  "tool_call": null
 },
